  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in from the west through \nmost of this week with sunny skies. Building hts as upper ridge\nstrengthens sends temps soaring through the 80s and nearing 90\nfor some interior locales (record high climate section below). \nA dry back door front is taking its time moving into nrn parts \nof ENC later today keeping most locations in the warm sector\ntoday with some increasing cloud cover and slightly cooler \ntemps for extreme northern locales. The front quickly retreats \nnorth by early Friday with another surge of swrly flow ahead of \nmuch stronger front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nUncertainty remains above average in regards to a coastal low\nforming along the cold front which will pass through the area\nlate Friday night into early Saturday morning. While there are\nstill three main camps as far as solutions go, confidence is\ntrending up that a coastal low will form. The location and\nstrength of that low remains in some question. \n\nOnce the low forms out over the gulf stream there are three main\nsteering paths that models are hinting at right now. The most\nlikely scenario at this time is that the low forms and stays\nover the gulf stream picking up strengths as it does so but\nremains in a fairly constant longitudinal range offshore. Most\nEPS ensemble members and some deterministic models such as the \nICON, CMC and others fall into this camp but there is still\nconsiderable spread in the location and intensity details. \n\nOther members along with the EC-AIFS model develop the low right\nalong the coast and hold it there for a much more prolonged\nevent. This scenario would likely have the highest impacts to\ncoastal areas of eastern NC with several days of strong winds\nand high waves.\n\nFinally, the GFS and its ensemble members remain an outlier in\neither not developing a low at all or developing it much later\nand much further east than any of the other scenarios. This\nremains the least likely to occur at this time.\n\nRegardless of which scenario plays out, there is HIGH confidence\nof marine impacts, and potential for waves 7+ ft reaching\nportions of the OBX. Winds advisory potential exists for the \nOBX zones, as well as perhaps portions of the adjacent coastal \nmainland. Will have to watch for minor coastal flood concerns \nfor srn portions of Pamlico Sound and the coastal areas of the \nOBX with the sustained NE flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coolest and driest airmass for the fall season \nthus far builds in behind the front Sat night and beyond with \ntemps taking a nosedive with highs in the 60s and lows in the \n40s (inland) and 50s (beaches). However, depending how offshore \nthe front makes it, could keep things a bit milder than fcst if \nsome clouds advect in from offshore keeping the nighttime mins \na bit warmer than advertised.",
